hypotheses.statement:
Let $f_1,\ldots,f_s$ be a finite list of genuine rational functions on $V$, whose length may vary with the sequence, and let $$\label{eq:C-neutral-polarization}
 J(T)=\max\{0,\max_j(-T(f_j))\},\qquad
 0\leq J\leq C L_0\mathbf D,\qquad1\leq L_0=O(L).$$

outputs.exact_statement:
1.  *Let $u_i$ lie in a fixed nonnegative orthant and $\lvert u_i\rvert\to\infty$. After passing to a subsequence there are $s_i\to0^+$, nonzero nonnegative integral vectors $n_i$, and a fixed, possibly subsequence-dependent, $C_*$ such that $$\label{eq:A-horizontal-approximation}
     \lvert n_i-s_i u_i\rvert\le C_*s_i,
     \qquad s_i\lvert u_i\rvert\longrightarrow\infty .$$*

2.  *Let $M_i x_i=r_i$, where $x_i\ge0$, $r_i$ is integral and $M_i$ belongs to a fixed finite list of integral matrices. If $\alpha_i>0$ tends to zero, then after passing to a subsequence there are positive integers $E_i$, nonnegative integral $n_i$, and a fixed $C_*$ such that $$\label{eq:A-vertical-approximation}
     M_i n_i=r_i E_i,\qquad
     s_i=E_i\alpha_i\longrightarrow0^+,\qquad
     \lvert n_i-E_i x_i\rvert\le C_*s_i .$$*


3.  *For every sufficiently small fixed $\gamma>0$, the same matching equations admit $n_i\ge0$ integral and $1\le E_i\le J(\gamma)$ with $$\label{eq:A-bounded-dirichlet}
     \lvert n_i-E_i x_i\rvert\le\gamma ,$$ where $J(\gamma)$ is uniform in $i$ and in the contact vectors $r_i$.

Let $F_i$ be projective of positive bounded dimension and let $G_i$ be ample $\mathbb Q$-Cartier rational divisors such that $(F_i,R(G_i))$ is klt and $$-(K_{F_i}+R(G_i))\sim_{\mathbb Q}I_iG_i,
 \qquad I_i>0,\quad I_i\longrightarrow\infty.$$ Let $C_i$ be the compactified Seifert cone obtained by contracting the zero section, and denote its infinity divisor by $D_i$. Then $C_i$ is klt of Fano type, $D_i$ is ample, and $-K_{C_i}\sim_{\mathbb Q}(I_i+1)D_i$. It is impossible that $$\label{eq:B-cone-small-height}
 v_i(D_i)=o\bigl(A_{C_i}(v_i)\bigr)$$ for every sequence of primitive divisorial valuations with $A_{C_i}(v_i)\to0$.

Assume <a href="#eq:C-alpha" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:C-alpha">[eq:C-alpha]</a> with $\eta_i\to0$ whenever $a(T)=0$ and $LD(T)\to0$, along arbitrary subsequences. Let $\ell$ be a rational principal order. Suppose that for every nonfloor prime $Q$ on $V$ there is an integer $p_Q$ with $$\label{eq:C-nonfloor-phase}
 |p_Q-\ell(Q)|\leq G(Q),\qquad
 G\geq0,\qquad G\sim_\mathbb Qc_gLD,$$ where $c_g\geq0$ is rational and uniformly bounded. There is a fixed positive integer $q$ such that $$\label{eq:C-scaled-phase}
 \operatorname{dist}(q\ell(T),\mathbb Z)=O(LD(T))
 \quad\text{if }a(T)=0\text{ and }LD(T)\longrightarrow0.$$

In the above setting the following statements hold.*

1.  *The discrepancy of $(X,H)$ at $(P,y)$ is $a(P)$, and its discrepancy at a pure weight is zero. Thus $a(P)$ is a nonnegative integer for every primitive $P$, and $\mathbf u\in\operatorname{int}C$.*



2.  *For rational $\mu\in\operatorname{int}C$, the ray quotient $V_\mu$ is normal and projective, with function field $\mathcal K$. The order function $$\label{eq:D-polarization}
     \mathbf L(\mu)(P)=\min_{y\in\Delta_P}\langle y,\mu\rangle$$ is the b-pullback of an ample rational divisor on $V_\mu$. Consequently $$\label{eq:D-height-divisor}
     \mathbf D(P)=\min_{y\in\Delta_P}(P,y)(h)
                 =\gamma(P)+\mathbf L(\mathbf u)(P)$$ is the b-pullback of an effective ample rational divisor $D$ on $V_{\mathbf u}$.*



3.  *At every actual prime $P$ of $V_\mu$, a minimizer $y$ of <a href="#eq:D-polarization" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:D-polarization">[eq:D-polarization]</a> can be chosen so that $e(P,y)$ is a primitive prime order on $X$, for some positive integer $e$. For this choice, $$\label{eq:D-actual-prime}
     a(P)+(P,y)(h)=\frac1e,
     \qquad e y\in\operatorname{Hom}(M,\mathbb Z).$$ On each $V_\mu$, $a$ is the discrepancy function of an effective ordinary lc Calabi–Yau pair, and $a+\mathbf D$ is that of an effective generalized klt Calabi–Yau pair with nef part $\mathbf D$. Each $V_\mu$ is of Fano type. At an actual prime outside the floor $\{a=0\}$, the same minimizer satisfies $$\label{eq:D-off-floor}
     a(P)=1,\qquad e=1,\qquad (P,y)(h)=0.$$

For every positive integer $q$ and every $g\in\mathcal K$, $$\label{eq:D-symbol-equivalence}
 g\in S(q\mathbf J_m)
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 g\xi^q h^{qm}\in\breve R.$$ In particular the neutral functions of cost $b\in\mathbb N$ are precisely $$\label{eq:D-neutral-functions}
 \{g\in\mathcal K:g h^b\in\breve R\}=S(b\mathbf D).$$

Let $L\ge1$ vary with the sequence. Choose integers $m>m_0$ with $m-m_0\ge cL$ for a fixed $c>0$, and suppose both $k+m_0\mathbf u$ and $k+m\mathbf u$ are interior. Assume that whenever primitive divisorial valuations $P$ satisfy $a(P)=0$ and $L\mathbf D(P)\to0$, there are integers $j_P$ such that $$\label{eq:D-sandwich-hypothesis}
 j_P\le\mathbf J_{m_0}(P)\le\mathbf J_m(P)
       \le j_P+O(L\mathbf D(P)).$$ Then there is a bounded positive integer $q$ and a nonzero $g\in S(q\mathbf J_m)$. Equivalently, $g\xi^qh^{qm}$ is regular on $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}\breve R$.

Assume the hypotheses of Lemma <a href="#lem:C-saturation-count" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:C-saturation-count">18</a> on $V_{\mathbf u}$, and use its notation $p$ and $v_i$. For a fixed $C_0>0$, let $W_{t,k}$ be the space of regular symbols in $\breve R$ of finite-group character $\chi^t$ and torus weight $t\mathbf u+k$. For every fixed positive integer $n$, eventually $$\label{eq:D-character-bound}
 \dim_k W_{t,k}\le C_3 v_i n^p$$ simultaneously for all integers $0\le t\le C_0nL$ and all weights satisfying $$|\langle y,k\rangle|\le C_0nL\langle y,\mathbf u\rangle
 \qquad(y\in\sigma).$$ The constant $C_3$ is independent of $n,t,k$.

Suppose there are boundedly many invariant rational eigenfunctions $Y_j^\pm$ of weights $\pm k_j$, with $$h^{m_j}Y_j^\pm\in\breve R,\qquad
 0<m_j\le A L_j,\qquad 1\le L_j\le L,$$ where $A$ is fixed. Fix bounded real numbers $u_j$ and $\delta>0$. Assume that, at every nonzero pure weight and at all Gauss extensions over primitive $P$ with $a(P)=0$, $L\mathbf D(P)\to0$, the evaluations satisfy $$\label{eq:D-tilt-hypothesis}
 y_j=v(Y_j^+)=-v(Y_j^-),\qquad
 \sum_j\frac{u_jy_j}{L_j}\le\delta v(h).$$ It suffices to check these inequalities on rational vectors. For fixed $M_0$ sufficiently large compared with $1/\delta$, choose integers $$r_j=M_0u_jL/L_j+O(1),$$ with uniformly bounded rounding errors. Then there is a regular nonzero symbol of finite-group character $\chi^{qm}$ and torus weight $$\label{eq:D-tilt-weight}
 q\left(m\mathbf u-\sum_jr_jk_j\right),
 \qquad 0<m\le C_6M_0\delta L,$$ for a bounded positive integer $q$. The constant $C_6$ depends only on the fixed bounds for the number of functions, their costs, the $u_j$, and rounding errors; it is independent of $\delta$ and $M_0$. The bound for $q$ may depend on the fixed choices $\delta,M_0$. No order condition <a href="#eq:C-alpha" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:C-alpha">[eq:C-alpha]</a> is assumed here.

The slice $\mathcal Q$ is a compact finite polyhedral complex of monomial valuations on any log resolution adapted to $h$ and the $F_j$. Evaluations of fixed functions are continuous on it. For centred valuations with $A(v)=1$, there are constants $c,C>0$ depending on the germ such that $$\label{eq:E-centering}
 \mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(v)\ge c,\qquad
 \mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(v)\ge \frac{c}{v(\mathfrak m)}.$$ Volume is continuous on the centred part of $\mathcal Q$.

Let $w$ be centred, let $v$ be over $x$, and suppose $u\ge tw+(1-t)v$ on $R$ for $0<t<1$. For $f\ne0$ with $w(f)>0$, $$\label{eq:E-volume-interpolation}
 \mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(u)\le t^{-n}
   \frac{tw(f)}{tw(f)+(1-t)v(f)}\mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(w).$$ If $v$ is also centred, $t=1/2$, and $v(f)\ge(1+\gamma)w(f)>0$ for $0<\gamma\le1$, then $$\label{eq:E-volume-gap}
 \mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(w)+\mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(v)-2\mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(u)\ge c(n,\gamma)\mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(w),$$ where $c(n,\gamma)>0$ depends only on $n,\gamma$. Consequently valuations whose volumes are within relative $o(1)$ of the minimum on the same $A=1$ common slice are multiplicatively $1+o(1)$ equal on *all* regular functions. The conclusion is uniform when the dimension is bounded. A sufficiently small fixed relative error similarly gives any prescribed fixed multiplicative closeness to one.

Choose positive rational numbers $\theta_j$ with $\sum_j\theta_j<1$. For sufficiently general scalars $a_j$, put $E_j=\mathop{\mathrm{div}}(h^{m_j}+a_jF_j)$ and $$\label{eq:E-slice-boundary}
 \Psi=(1-\sum_j\theta_j)H+
                     \sum_j\frac{\theta_j}{m_j}E_j.$$ Then $\Psi$ is effective and lc, and its nonzero zero-discrepancy rays over the germ are exactly the common lc rays. On every such ray, $$\label{eq:E-pencil-order}
 v(h^{m_j}+a_jF_j)=m_jv(h).$$ In particular, the coefficients $\theta_j$ may be chosen arbitrarily small while retaining these conclusions.

For the minimizing ray $w$ and every $v\in\mathcal Q$, $$\label{eq:E-domination}
 \frac{v(f)}{b(v)}\le 2^n\frac{w(f)}{b(w)}
                 \qquad(f\in R).$$ For rational heights the same statement holds on the central slice with all symbols carrying a superscript $0$.

Consider a sequence of the preceding data in dimensions at most $n_0$. Suppose that the common actual slices satisfy $q(f_j)=0$ identically, that $w$ is a centred point of the slice with the stipulated normal finitely generated klt graded algebra, and that the cones satisfy the following balance condition with one constant $C$ along the sequence: $$\label{eq:F-balance}
 q^0(s)\le Cw^0(s)
 \quad\begin{gathered}
 0\ne s\in\breve R\text{ fine-homogeneous},\\[-2pt]
 q^0\text{ common},\quad q^0(\bar h)=w(h)=1.
 \end{gathered}$$ After passage to a subsequence, the $w$ can be replaced by $w'$ with all these properties, with a new fixed balance constant, and with $$\label{eq:F-refinement-comparison}
 w'(f)=(1+o(1))w(f)
 \qquad(0\ne f\in\mathcal O_{S,x}),$$ uniformly in $f$ in the normalization $w'(h)=w(h)=1$. On the angular model $V_{\mathbf u}$ of the new cone, the notation of Section <a href="#sec:D" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:D">4</a> then satisfies $$\label{eq:F-angular-order}
 T(\Gamma)\le(1+\eta_i)D(T),\qquad \eta_i\longrightarrow0,$$ for every effective $\mathbb Q$-divisor $\Gamma\sim_\mathbb QD$ and every primitive divisorial $T$ such that $a(T)=0$ and $T(\bar f_j)\ge0$ for all $j$. This is condition <span class="upright">(C-$\alpha$)</span> of Section <a href="#sec:C" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:C">3</a>. The initial symbols here refer to $w'$.*

*The balance hypothesis holds when $w$ is the exact minimizer on the common slice for the height $q(h)$.

Suppose $f$ is not an isomorphism near $z$. The signed algebra $$\label{eq:H-signed-algebra}
 R=\bigoplus_{k\in\mathbb Z}
       H^0(X,\mathcal O_X(-kK_X))t^k,\qquad S_0=\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}R,$$ over the affine base is a finitely generated normal domain of dimension $\dim X+1$. Write $R_k=H^0(X,\mathcal O_X(-kK_X))t^k$ for its homogeneous part of degree $k$. Its *positive chamber* is the open $$S_0^+=\bigcup_{k>0}\ \bigcup_{0\ne a\in R_k}D_{S_0}(a),$$ where $D_{S_0}(a)$ is the principal open on which $a$ does not vanish. This chamber has complement of codimension at least two. The form $$\label{eq:H-torsor-form}
 \Omega_0=t\omega_X\wedge d\log t$$ is a divisor-free canonical generator of grading weight one, and every finite scalar grading subgroup acts quasi-étalely.*


*The variety $S_0$ is klt, and $(S_0,\mathop{\mathrm{div}}(s_bt^b)/b)$ is lc. There is a grading-fixed closed point $o$ over $z$ and a rational Gauss valuation $w_0$ centred there which satisfies <a href="#eq:H-upstairs-saturation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:H-upstairs-saturation">[eq:H-upstairs-saturation]</a> for every actual positive degree $k\mid M_i$. Moreover there are numbers $\alpha>0$ and $F_w>\alpha$, depending on the example, for which every nonzero homogeneous section satisfies $$\label{eq:H-two-slopes}
 w_0(s t^k)\ge
 \begin{cases}
 k\alpha,&k>0,\\
 |k|(F_w-\alpha),&k<0.
 \end{cases}$$

In either construction, set $$S=S_0\times\mathbf A^{b-1},
 \qquad (o,0)\in S,$$ and give every added coordinate $u_j$ character one. Then $S$ is klt, the finite quotient $Y=S/\mu_r$ is $\epsilon$-lc, and the canonical generator on $S$ has character $b$. There is a regular function $h$ of character $b$ such that $(S,\mathop{\mathrm{div}}(h))$ is lc and a centred quasi-monomial valuation $T_0$ such that $$\label{eq:H-padding-equality}
 A_S(T_0)=T_0(h)=bA_0.$$
